CAT = "oacpVocab"; SEG = "VOC"
Q = [
("In the sentence \"A second witness corroborated the driver's account,\" the word corroborated most nearly means:", "confirmed", ["contradicted", "recorded", "questioned"], "To corroborate is to support or confirm with more evidence. Contradicted is the reverse: it means the second witness disagreed."),
("In the sentence \"The belligerent customer refused to leave the bar,\" the word belligerent most nearly means:", "hostile", ["nervous", "talkative", "confused"], "Belligerent means aggressive or ready to fight. Someone nervous may also refuse to move, but nervous does not carry the sense of aggression."),
("In the sentence \"The officer believed an assault was imminent,\" the word imminent most nearly means:", "about to happen", ["already finished", "extremely serious", "widely known"], "Imminent means about to happen. Widely known confuses it with eminent, which means famous or respected."),
("In the sentence \"The release of the victim's name was inadvertent,\" the word inadvertent most nearly means:", "accidental", ["deliberate", "unlawful", "unavoidable"], "Inadvertent means done by accident, without meaning to. Unavoidable is tempting, but an inadvertent act could have been avoided; it simply was not intended."),
("In the sentence \"Despite the head injury, the victim was lucid,\" the word lucid most nearly means:", "clear-headed", ["half-asleep", "agitated", "uncooperative"], "Lucid means thinking and speaking clearly. Half-asleep describes a common symptom after a head injury, but the word despite signals the opposite of what was expected."),
("In the sentence \"The suspect issued a vehement denial,\" the word vehement most nearly means:", "forceful", ["hesitant", "written", "partial"], "Vehement means showing strong feeling or force. Hesitant is close to the opposite, describing a weak or uncertain denial."),
("In the sentence \"The witness gave a candid account of her own role,\" the word candid most nearly means:", "open and honest", ["short and vague", "badly confused", "rehearsed ahead"], "Candid means open and honest, even about uncomfortable facts. Rehearsed ahead suggests the opposite: an account prepared in advance rather than given openly."),
("In the sentence \"Visible patrols can deter break-ins,\" the word deter most nearly means:", "discourage", ["encourage", "investigate", "detect"], "To deter is to discourage someone from acting. Detect sounds similar but means to discover something already happening."),
("In the sentence \"Officers apprehended the suspect near the bridge,\" the word apprehended most nearly means:", "arrested", ["identified", "pursued", "questioned"], "In police use, to apprehend is to arrest or take into custody. Pursued describes the chase before the arrest, not the arrest itself."),
("In the sentence \"She was charged in the alleged theft,\" the word alleged most nearly means:", "claimed, not proven", ["already confirmed", "only attempted", "recently reported"], "Alleged means said to have happened but not yet proven. Already confirmed is wrong because a charge does not prove the theft occurred."),
("In the sentence \"The meaning of the note left at the scene was ambiguous,\" the word ambiguous most nearly means:", "unclear", ["threatening", "unsigned", "obvious"], "Ambiguous means open to more than one meaning. Obvious is the opposite, since an obvious note has only one clear meaning."),
("In the sentence \"Wearing a seat belt can mitigate injuries in a crash,\" the word mitigate most nearly means:", "lessen", ["worsen", "diagnose", "conceal"], "To mitigate is to make less severe. Worsen is the opposite and is a common mix-up with the similar-sounding aggravate."),
("In the sentence \"The stolen car was conspicuous in the empty lot,\" the word conspicuous most nearly means:", "easy to notice", ["well hidden", "badly damaged", "parked illegally"], "Conspicuous means standing out and easy to see. Well hidden is its opposite."),
("In the sentence \"The sergeant noticed a discrepancy between the two reports,\" the word discrepancy most nearly means:", "difference", ["agreement", "similarity", "connection"], "A discrepancy is a difference or inconsistency between things that should match. Agreement and similarity mean the reports matched."),
("Which word is most nearly opposite in meaning to coherent?", "muddled", ["logical", "lengthy", "honest"], "Coherent means clear and logically connected, so muddled is its opposite. Logical is a synonym of coherent, not an opposite."),
("Which word is most nearly opposite in meaning to compliant?", "defiant", ["obedient", "careless", "nervous"], "A compliant person follows directions; a defiant person openly refuses. Obedient is a synonym of compliant, not an opposite."),
("Which word is most nearly opposite in meaning to impartial?", "biased", ["neutral", "absent", "modest"], "Impartial means treating all sides equally, and biased means favouring one side. Neutral is a synonym of impartial."),
("Which word is most nearly opposite in meaning to culpable?", "blameless", ["responsible", "talkative", "fearful"], "Culpable means deserving blame, so blameless is the opposite. Responsible, in the sense of being to blame, is close to culpable."),
("Which word is most nearly opposite in meaning to obstruct?", "assist", ["hinder", "observe", "report"], "To obstruct is to block or get in the way; to assist is to help. Hinder is close to obstruct in meaning."),
("Which word is most nearly opposite in meaning to lenient?", "strict", ["merciful", "lazy", "forgetful"], "Lenient means mild in applying rules or punishment, so strict is the opposite. Merciful is close to lenient."),
("Which word is most nearly opposite in meaning to reticent?", "talkative", ["reserved", "honest", "tired"], "A reticent person holds back from speaking, so talkative is the opposite. Reserved is a synonym of reticent."),
("Which word is most nearly opposite in meaning to transient?", "permanent", ["fleeting", "sudden", "distant"], "Transient means lasting only a short time, so permanent is the opposite. Fleeting means much the same as transient."),
("Which word is most nearly opposite in meaning to sporadic?", "constant", ["occasional", "careless", "loud"], "Sporadic means happening now and then, without a pattern, so constant is the opposite. Occasional is a synonym of sporadic."),
("Which word is most nearly opposite in meaning to credible?", "dubious", ["reliable", "famous", "nervous"], "Credible means believable, and dubious means doubtful and hard to believe. Reliable is close to credible, not its opposite."),
("Which word best completes the report: \"The suspect's ___ changed from calm to agitated when the dog arrived.\"?", "demeanour", ["diligence", "discretion", "deference"], "Demeanour is a person's outward behaviour or manner, which can change from calm to agitated. Discretion means good judgment about what to say or do, not outward manner."),
("Which word best completes the report: \"The driver appeared ___: slurred speech, an unsteady walk and a strong odour of alcohol.\"?", "intoxicated", ["intimidated", "incarcerated", "intimated"], "Intoxicated means affected by alcohol or drugs, which matches the signs listed. Intimidated means frightened, which the signs do not describe."),
("Which word best completes the report: \"The officer tried to ___ the man from climbing onto the ledge.\"?", "dissuade", ["persuade", "dismiss", "disperse"], "To dissuade is to talk someone out of doing something, and it takes from. Persuade means to talk someone into something and would need to, not from."),
("Which word best completes the report: \"Before releasing the vehicle, the clerk must ___ the owner's identity.\"?", "verify", ["vilify", "ratify", "testify"], "To verify is to check that something is true or accurate. Vilify means to speak about someone in an abusive way, a look-alike with an unrelated meaning."),
("Which word best completes the report: \"A statement is not voluntary if officers ___ the suspect into giving it.\"?", "coerce", ["concur", "convey", "condone"], "To coerce is to force someone by threats or pressure. Condone means to accept or overlook wrongdoing, which does not fit into giving it."),
("Which word best completes the report: \"Officers may briefly ___ a person while they investigate a nearby robbery.\"?", "detain", ["retain", "obtain", "attain"], "To detain is to hold a person temporarily. Retain means to keep something, such as a file or a lawyer, not to hold a person."),
("Which word best completes the report: \"Officers must stay ___ throughout a long overnight stakeout.\"?", "vigilant", ["vigorous", "virulent", "vindictive"], "Vigilant means watchful and alert for danger. Vigorous means energetic and strong, which is not the same as watching carefully."),
("Which word best completes the report: \"The man was ___ that he had never met the victim and would not change his answer.\"?", "adamant", ["uncertain", "apathetic", "amenable"], "Adamant means firm and unwilling to change one's mind. Uncertain is close to the opposite: unsure and open to doubt."),
]
